<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ifá</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ifá is a system of divination that originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, within the traditional religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a>. According to Ifá teaching it is overseen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> Orunmila, who is believed to have given it to humanity. Its initiates, called babaláwo if male and ìyánífá if female, consult a body of 256 signs, the Odù, using a divining chain or palm nuts on a wooden divination tray, the ọpọ́n Ifá. Yoruba influence spread Ifá to neighbouring peoples such as the Fon, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> carried it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where it survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ifa.jpg" alt="Ifá" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toluaye · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jogo_de_Ikin_Orossi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition holds that Ifá began at Ilé-Ifẹ̀, where Orunmila initiated himself and then his students, Akoda and Aseda. Samuel Johnson and Obadiah Johnson wrote in 1921 that Arugba, mother of Onibogi, the 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, brought Ifá to Oyo in the late 1400s. The linguist Wande Abimbola argued that it probably grew out of a simpler system, dilogun. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> it spread across lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>; the Fon adopted it while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Oyo Empire, and it was well established at the royal palace under Gezò.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sixteen major Odù combine into 256 signs, which believers hold to cover every situation in life through countless ese, poetic teachings that are passed down orally from one babalawo to another and never written. Eshu-Elegba is held to carry Ifá's will to humanity and to the other spirit-deities. Among the Fon, where the system is called Fá and the word evokes coolness and calm, the female spirit Gbădu, Fá's wife, is regarded as the source of its power. A divining chain may be made of eight nut halves tied together, and its fall gives one of the 256 signs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Traditionally only men became babalawos, though women have been initiated, and by the early 21st century female practitioners called iyanifá were becoming institutionalised in some communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> a priestly initiation takes place in Fá's sacred forest, where the initiate's sign is divined and fixes the taboos he must keep. In Cuba Ifá became the most complex and prestigious divination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a>; an estimated 200 babalawos there in 1959 had grown to tens of thousands by the 1990s, by their own claims.</p><p>Cuban migrants carried Ifá to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where the Nigerian babalawo Ifayẹmi Elébùìbọn led ceremonies in Miami in 1978, and Nigerian teachers ran an Ifá course in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1977, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> uses dilogun instead. A church founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1934, the Ijọ Ọ̀rúnmila Adulawọ, treated Ifá's teachings as the Yoruba Bible. UNESCO proclaimed Ifá a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ifá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yoruba divination by <b>Ifá</b> was characterised by the start of the 21st century as either extinct or very rare in Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/religion/candomble">Candomblé · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ifá</b>, the most complex and prestigious system of divination used in the religion, has a separate existence from Santería but shares its mythology and conception of the universe.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
